{{Trail|Creatures|Undead}}[[Category:Skyrim-Creatures-All]][[Category:Skyrim-Creatures-Undead-Draugr]]{{TOCright|limit=3}}
[[File:SR-quest-Evil in Waiting 03.jpg|thumb|left|A draugr using the thu'um]][[File:SR-creature-Draugr Deathlord female.jpg|thumb|right|A female draugr]]
'''Draugr''' are ancient Nordic warriors who have been reanimated. Most draugr were followers of [[Skyrim:Dragon Priest|dragon priests]] in ancient times. When these priests died, their followers were killed, reanimated, and then sealed into the tomb where they would periodically awaken in order to worship the priests and transfer energy to them to maintain their undead state. Some draugr instead guard nobility and treasures buried in [[Skyrim:Nordic Ruins|barrows and tombs]]. They often rest in catacombs or sarcophagi to awaken and fight when disturbed or alerted.
{{NewLeft}}

==Appearance and Types==
Despite the advanced embalming techniques of the ancient Nords, time has taken its toll on draugr's physical appearance. Their soft tissues are severely decomposed, and their armor does little to conceal the fact that much of their flesh is rotting away, exposing the underlying skeletal structure. All draugr have piercing blue eyes. Male and female draugr appear at similar rates, but at higher levels, males far outnumber females. Higher rank draugr are clearly recognizable by their increasingly more impressive helmets and equipment. The Draugr Deathlords and Death Overlords will almost always carry [[Skyrim:Ebony|ebony]] equipment and are most recognizable by their unobtainable versions of the [[Skyrim:Ancient Nord Helmet|ancient Nord helmets]].

==Characteristics==
Most draugr are [[Skyrim:Resist Poison|immune to poison]] and have a 50% [[Skyrim:Resist Frost|resistance to frost]]. However, the Hallowed Dead found in [[Skyrim:Labyrinthian|Labyrinthian]] are not immune to poison and are 33% [[Skyrim:Weakness to Magic|weaker to magic]].

The specific variety of draugr that you meet is generally level-dependent. However, this also depends on the location at which they're encountered. In addition, draugr may be spellcasters, archers, one-handed melee fighters, or two-handed melee fighters; this is unspecified by their names, which only indicate their class. Draugr that begin appearing early in the game are equipped with [[Skyrim:Specialty Gear#Ancient Nord Weapons|ancient Nordic weapons]], which are similar in strength to steel weaponry, and [[Skyrim:Ancient Nord Armor|ancient Nord armor]], similar in quality to iron armor, which is unlootable. The more powerful draugr that appear at higher levels often use [[Skyrim:Ebony|ebony]] weapons and equipment, and may have the ability to use one or more [[Skyrim:Dragon Shouts|dragon shouts]] in combat, including [[Skyrim:Unrelenting Force|Unrelenting Force]], [[Skyrim:Disarm|Disarm]], and sometimes even [[Skyrim:Frost Breath|Frost Breath]]. Most types of draugr, apart from the very weakest, will employ [[Skyrim:Frost Damage|Frost Damage]] in combat, whether through spells, shouts, or enchanted weapons. Draugr have a high ratio of health to their level when compared to most enemy types, such as [[Skyrim:Vampire|Vampires]] and enemy mages, making them all the more dangerous. 

You will often encounter draugr enthralled by [[Skyrim:Necromancer|necromancers]] or hostile mages, such as those found in the Nordic ruin of [[Skyrim:Ansilvund|Ansilvund]] where the draugr are forced to mine.

For information about dead, static draugr which can be looted, see [[Skyrim:Containers#Draugr|this page]].

===Combat Strategies===
Eliminate weaker types before fighting the stronger ones. Archers can deal a lot of damage during a battle, but they often shoot from a distance, sometimes making it easy to hide or take cover from them while dealing with nearer enemies. Stronger draugr can be very dangerous to most players, and should be attacked from a distance when possible. If you are a mage, avoid the use of frost spells, but concentrate on the two remaining elements: flames to constantly inflict damage, and shock to drain the enemy's magicka pool. Flaming Familiars, Flame Atronachs and Storm Atronachs will be particularly potent.

You can often perform a [[Skyrim:Sneak#Sneak Attacks|sneak attack]] on a draugr sleeping in its alcove and kill it before it is able to attack. Even if you fail to kill it immediately, you should have time for one or two more hits while it climbs out of its resting place. [[Skyrim:Sneak#Remaining Undetected|Remaining undetected]], even by sleeping draugr, can be challenging, as they are quite sensitive, and this increases with stronger versions. [[Skyrim:Dragon Priest|Dragon priests]], who guard some Nordic ruins, are extremely dangerous opponents, but can often be defeated from a distance by using the [[Skyrim:Invisibility (effect)|Invisibility]] and [[Skyrim:Muffle (effect)|Muffle]] effects to evade detection.

You can lure draugr into many of the [[Skyrim:Traps|traps]] common in Nordic ruins, which can be very effective. [[Skyrim:Dart Trap|Dart traps]] are not helpful in this regard, as most draugr are [[Skyrim:Resist Poison|immune to poison]].

When engaging with high level draugr that use the [[Skyrim:Unrelenting Force|Unrelenting Force]] and [[SR:Disarm|Disarm]] shouts, you may wish to save frequently in case a treasured weapon is thrown and you cannot find it again. [[Skyrim:Ward|Wards]] (including the ward effect that the unique shield [[Skyrim:Spellbreaker|Spellbreaker]] provides when blocking) will render any shout harmless, from draugr or other sources. 

Draugr are unable to attack and have no defenses while the animation plays as they emerge from their tomb.  Attacking them during this time can quickly eliminate them before they can strike.
